New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President. And thank you, Under‑Secretary-General DiCarlo, for your informative briefing.
Colleagues, every day that this conflict persists is unacceptable.
Civilians are being killed across Sudan—not only by the lethal weapons and mercenaries that continue flowing into the country—but also by hunger and health concerns exacerbated by the conflict and the expanding risk of famine.
We need a humanitarian truce. A humanitarian truce would alleviate the persistent suffering of the Sudanese people, and we will continue to work with the parties to achieve that end.
However, peace efforts and political efforts need to be mutually reinforcing; we cannot have real dialogue that includes civilians from across Sudan, and those sheltering abroad, if we do not have a humanitarian truce.
That is why the Quintet’s work to establish an inclusive political dialogue is so critical.
And let me just say, we really appreciate the work of Mr. Haavisto on this file.
Mr. Haavisto’s leadership of the Quintet cannot be overstated, because the Quintet-led effort is the most viable and credible path to lay the foundation for a peaceful political transition to civilian-led governance.
This Council should clearly and emphatically support the establishment of a political process that focuses on achieving a comprehensive political settlement and a unified Sudan that preserves state institutions and is accountable to its people.
Any national political dialogue must be Sudanese owned and led. It must be free from interference by violent extremist groups linked to internationally designated terrorist groups, as well as those who have committed atrocities against the Sudanese people.
We must all reject efforts to conduct forum shopping or establish competing political dialogues.
It is time to restore peace and give power back to the Sudanese people.
But in order to empower the Sudanese people and to transition to a civilian-led government, this Council must take concrete action to stop the never-ending flow of weapons into the country.
Drone attacks and air strikes continue to kill and injure scores of civilians every day, with no sign of slowing.
Just last week, we saw a horrific strike on a displacement shelter in Blue Nile State, reportedly by the RSF, that killed more than 30 internally displaced persons.
Days before, an attack on a World Food Program (WFP) convoy killed a driver – the ninth aerial attack on WFP operations in Sudan this year alone. This must stop.
An expanded arms embargo will raise the cost of continued conflict and impede the ability of external actors to provide military support to the warring parties.
The United States will continue to push to expand the sanctions regime to better reflect the realities of a conflict that now spans the entire country.
We must also address the deepening threat of severe food insecurity in parts of Sudan.
Over the course of this conflict, famine has been declared multiple times, and the risk of another famine declaration looms large.
As the harvest season begins in Sudan, we call on the parties to provide protection not only for civilians but also for food systems, including farmlands and markets, as well as the safe movement of food.
In the face of growing extreme food insecurity, an effective humanitarian response is critical. We continue to call for unhindered humanitarian access.
Aid workers need air access to Darfur now. They often spend days traveling over land to Darfur.
Air access will reduce their travel time from days to hours, and will give humanitarian organizations the ability to conduct medical evacuations for their staff, enabling a greater humanitarian presence.
Aid workers also need more time on the ground in Darfur. Currently, the travel permits that UN staff receive are too short in duration and they experience long wait times when seeking to renew them.
These administrative constraints are hampering the humanitarian response, and we urge the Sudanese authorities to provide timely, regular access to Darfur.
Finally, despite the ongoing conflict, some refugees and IDPs are making the decision to return.
Upon return, they face many challenges, including damaged infrastructure, lack of basic services, unexploded ordnances, and in some cases finding their home or land occupied by others.
Recognizing the determination and resilience—and I’ve had the privilege to meet dozens of these refugees personally earlier this year. I can say the bravery of these returnees, we call on the parties to facilitate the free, safe, and voluntary return of refugees and IDPs, including the return of property, when safe to do so.
The United States stands ready to work with each Member of this Council to support the Quintet’s efforts to advance an inclusive civilian political process and reinforce efforts to achieve a humanitarian truce and unhindered humanitarian access, which will ultimately save lives and prevent further bloodshed.
I thank you.
