Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President, and thank you, Special Representative Tetteh, for your briefing today.
The United States welcomes the historic August 30 agreement in Tripoli, facilitated by UNSMIL, to hold national elections under a unified executive.
In parallel, the United States has pursued sustained, hands-on diplomacy to support Libyan-led initiatives to overcome political divisions and unify Libya’s political, economic, and military institutions.
This engagement has produced tangible results, including Libya’s first unified national budget in 13 years and the joint Flintlock 2026 exercises, bringing east and west Libyan forces together under AFRICOM leadership.
Our efforts remain fully complementary to UNSMIL’s roadmap, and both tracks share the same goal: a unified executive and a credible path toward national elections that reflect the will of the Libyan people. It is imperative that Libyans move beyond political rivalry and instead faithfully implement the 4+4 agreement.
We note the House of Representatives’ selection of new leadership on October 6. We encourage Speaker Abdulnabi to work constructively with UNSMIL and finalize the electoral reform laws without delay.
We are concerned by the High Council of State’s political maneuver to disrupt progress toward elections by rejecting the 4+4 agreement and we call upon the body to uphold its commitment to the aforementioned agreement.
The UN Security Council has made it clear many times, and it bears repeating now: individuals or entities who obstruct or undermine the successful completion of its political transition, including elections, may be designated under Security Council sanctions and held accountable.
We remain deeply concerned by the recent events such as drone strikes on energy infrastructure and resulting blackouts, water service disruptions, and protests across Tripoli, Zawiya, and Misrata.
We are similarly troubled by the September blockade of the Sharara-Zawiya pipeline, which cost the Libyan people nearly $95 million in lost oil revenue.
We note UNSMIL’s reminder that continued attacks on energy infrastructure risk triggering sanctions, and we urge restraint from all parties. Vital energy facilities benefit all the people of Libya and they must not be targeted.
This recent instability highlights that the status quo is unsustainable and underscores the need for Libyan leaders to move swiftly to implement their plans for a unified executive and a path to elections, as outlined in the 4+4 agreement.
Again, let’s be clear: this Council and the international community must hold persons that obstruct or undermine progress toward elections in Libya to account, including by subjecting them to sanctions.
Sustained U.S. engagement, alongside UNSMIL’s continued efforts, offers a clear path to translate recent progress into lasting peace and unity.
We look forward to working with the Council to renew the mandate of UNSMIL while keeping the text focused and ensuring a responsible stewardship of UN resources.
Thank you.
