Ambassador Jennifer Locetta
Alternative Representative for Special Political Affairs
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Madam President. First, I would like to thank our co-penholder Panama for its constructive leadership on this resolution. The United States also stands with Panama in the aftermath of the earthquake that struck the country.
As Panama’s term on the Security Council nears its end, we’d like to also extend a warm thank you for two great years of collaboration as our co-penholder. We are deeply grateful for your dedication to peace and security in Haiti as we work together towards a more secure and prosperous Western Hemisphere.
We welcome the extension of the mandate of the Haiti Panel of Experts, and the renewal of the travel ban, asset freeze, and arms embargo measures for another year.
As Haiti continues the fight to regain control of its country, the United States wishes to make a clarifying point to all member states as it relates to the territorial arms embargo: the arms embargo does not apply to the sale, supply, or transfer, of arms and related materiel, or the provision of related assistance and training, to the Government of Haiti, intended solely to further objectives of peace and stability.
Today’s vote is yet another unwavering signal that the international community stands with the people of Haiti.
Just a few weeks ago, this Council reauthorized a novel mission that is uniquely designed to address immediate security threats in Haiti and to promote peace and stability in the country.
In that regard, the United States encourages the international community to support Haiti’s National Commission for Disarmament, Dismantlement, and Reintegration. This initiative will be key to tackling the gangs that are destabilizing the country, and offer a pathway towards peace.
Consistent with the resolution, we also call upon the Haitian government to adopt a decree regulating the exploitation of baby eels also known as elvers, and related trade. This illicit exploitation of this wildlife is fueling armed groups and criminal networks in the country and depriving the Haitian people of their resources.
The United States will also continue to consider designations through the Security Council and its subsidiary bodies to ensure UN sanctions lists are fit for purpose.
These sanctions are key components of a broader international effort to promote peace and stability in Haiti, and in the region.
Thank you.
