ATLANTA – Twenty individuals, including five members of the GoodFellas criminal street gang and four convicted felons, have been indicted on federal gun and drug charges, including firearms-trafficking charges arising from the theft of nearly 1,500 guns stolen from shipping facilities in Conley, Georgia and Macon, Georgia.

“Gang‑related firearms trafficking and drug trafficking pose a grave threat to our community,” said U.S. Attorney Theodore S. Hertzberg. “Criminal networks that move guns and narcotics fuel violence, undermine neighborhoods, and endanger innocent lives. Using every lawful tool available, my office and our law enforcement partners in the Homeland Security Task Force are committed to eliminating these criminal enterprises, holding offenders accountable, and protecting law-abiding Georgians.”

“Stealing nearly 1,500 firearms and flooding our communities with guns and drugs is more than brazen criminal conduct; it is a direct attack on public safety,” said ATF Atlanta Field Division Special Agent in Charge Benjamin Gibbons. “The GoodFellas gang and their associates allegedly put deadly firepower on the streets across multiple states and even beyond our borders. ATF will not allow criminal organizations to profit by endangering our communities. As this investigation shows, our agents, working with our federal, state, and local partners, move quickly to shut down illegal firearms pipelines. If you steal guns, traffic guns, or arm violent gangs, ATF will find you, we will dismantle your operation, and you will be held accountable.”

“The sheer amount of stolen guns and drugs these individuals were allegedly dealing with is alarming,” said Richard Bilson, Assistant Special Agent in Charge of FBI Atlanta. “The FBI will continue to prioritize investigations like these so that Georgians will be able to live without fear of either themselves or their loved ones becoming a victim of the violence used or drugs peddled by these street gangs and their associates.”

According to U.S. Attorney Hertzberg, the charges, and other information presented in court: The GoodFellas is an Atlanta-based gang that primarily recruits members in Atlanta neighborhoods as well as jails and prisons within Georgia. The gang generates money through, among other criminal activities, drug trafficking, robbery, carjacking, fraud, and firearms trafficking. The gang protects its operation, turf, and reputation through violence and threats of violence.

On November 29 and 30, 2025, Tremaine Snell, Tarik Winfrey, and Daveon Stillwell allegedly broke into a shipping facility in Conley, Georgia and stole 724 Glock pistols. On December 2, 2025, Devarrio Burns and Christopher Billings, both of whom are GoodFellas members, sold two of the stolen pistols to an undercover ATF agent.

On January 10, 2026, Snell allegedly broke into a shipping facility in Macon, Georgia and stole 469 firearms. On January 16, 2026, Snell and Stillwell sold an undercover ATF agent four firearms taken during the Conley and Macon burglaries.

On January 16, 2026, Snell, Winfrey, and GoodFellas member Marquez Crews allegedly broke into the same Macon shipping facility and stole 293 firearms. The next day, agents recovered three of the stolen firearms from Calvin Washington’s South Fulton residence. On January 23, 2026, Crews, assisted by Snell, Temeka Camiel, and Matthew Gaskin, sold 14 of the stolen firearms to an undercover ATF agent. On April 1, 2026, agents seized from convicted felon Shanteria Horton an eighteenth firearm taken from the Macon facility.

During a traffic stop on February 19, 2026, law enforcement encountered convicted felons Samdrekas Floyd and Antonio Thomas and allegedly seized firearms from both of them.

On May 13, 2026, the FBI, ATF, and other law enforcement agencies executed search warrants at 12 locations in the Atlanta area allegedly associated with the GoodFellas gang. Across all locations, agents seized approximately 24 kilograms of methamphetamine, 22 kilograms of marijuana, 40 grams of cocaine base, 27 grams of fentanyl, and a pill press used to manufacture counterfeit pills. Additionally, agents seized firearms from Samuel Harris, Joseph Sutton, Deanthony Cole, and Demontra Williams, all of whom are convicted felons.

On September 23, 2026, a federal grand jury returned six indictments charging the following defendants:

Christopher Billings, 36, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to traffic firearms, firearms trafficking, and possession of a firearm by a convicted felon;

Devarrio Burns, a/k/a Debo, 32, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute a controlled substance, conspiracy to traffic firearms, firearms trafficking, and possession of a firearm by a convicted felon;

Temeka Camiel, 50, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to traffic firearms and firearms trafficking;

Deanthony Cole, 33, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with possession of a firearm by a convicted felon;

Kordion Cousins, 29, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with transferring a firearm to an out-of-state resident;

Marquez Crews, a/k/a Quez, 25, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to traffic firearms, firearms trafficking, conspiracy to commit theft of interstate shipments, and theft of interstate shipments;

Brandon Elligan, a/k/a Mario World, a/k/a Billy Mario, 39, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute a controlled substance and possession of a firearm in furtherance of a drug trafficking offense;

Samdrekas Floyd, a/k/a Hot, 32, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with possession of a firearm by a convicted felon;

Matthew Gaskin, a/k/a Matthew Gaskins, 23, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to traffic firearms and firearms trafficking;

Samuel Harris, a/k/a Bobo, 41, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute a controlled substance, possession of a firearm by a convicted felon, and possession of a firearm in furtherance of a drug trafficking offense;

Shanteria Horton, 27 of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with possession of a firearm by a convicted felon;

Ramudo Lloyd, 52, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute a controlled substance;

Deavis Reese, a/k/a Boogaloo, a/k/a Unk, a/k/a Boo, 51, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute a controlled substance;

Tremaine Snell, a/k/a Big, a/k/a Man Man, 26, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to traffic firearms, firearms trafficking, conspiracy to commit theft of interstate shipments, and theft of interstate shipments;

Daveon Stillwell, a/k/a Drip, 21, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to traffic firearms, firearms trafficking, conspiracy to commit theft of interstate shipments, and theft of interstate shipments;

Joseph Sutton, a/k/a Squirt, a/k/a O, 43, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute a controlled substance, possession with intent to distribute a controlled substance, possession of a firearm by a convicted felon, and possession of a firearm in furtherance of a drug trafficking offense;

Antonio Thomas, 27, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with possession of a firearm by a convicted felon;

Calvin Washington, 28, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with unlawful possession of a stolen firearm;

Demontra Williams, 41, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute a controlled substance, possession of a firearm by a convicted felon, and possession of a firearm in furtherance of a drug trafficking offense; and

Tarik Winfrey, 26, of Atlanta, Georgia, is charged with conspiracy to traffic firearms, firearms trafficking, conspiracy to commit theft of interstate shipments, and theft of interstate shipments.

Earlier today, FBI agents arrested Deanthony Cole, Brandon Elligan, Ramudo Lloyd, Deavis Reese, and Joseph Sutton. On September 25, 2026, ATF agents arrested Temeka Camiel, Kordion Cousins, Marquez Crews, Tremaine Snell, and Calvin Washington. Defendants Christopher Billings, Samdrekas Floyd, Matthew Gaskin, Shanteria Horton, Daveon Stillwell, Antonio Thomas, and Tarik Winfrey are currently being held in local custody and will appear in federal court at a later date. Defendant Devarrio Burns was recently transferred from local to federal custody and will be arraigned later today.

Samuel Harris and Demontra Williams are currently fugitives. If you have any information on their whereabouts, please contact your local law enforcement agency.

Members of the public are reminded that the indictments only contain charges. The defendants are presumed innocent of the charges, and it will be the government’s burden to prove the defendants’ guilt beyond a reasonable doubt at trial.

This case is being investigated by the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives and the Federal Bureau of Investigation, with valuable assistance from the Atlanta Police Department, Bibb County Sheriff’s Office, Georgia Department of Community Supervision, Georgia State Police, Georgia Department of Corrections, and Georgia Bureau of Investigation’s West Metro Regional Drug Enforcement Office.

These prosecutions are part of the Homeland Security Task Force (HSTF) initiative established by Executive Order 14159, Protecting the American People Against Invasion. The HSTF is a whole-of-government partnership dedicated to eliminating criminal cartels, foreign gangs, transnational criminal organizations, and human smuggling and trafficking rings operating in the United States and abroad. Through historic interagency collaboration, the HSTF directs the full might of United States law enforcement towards identifying, investigating, and prosecuting the full spectrum of crimes committed by these organizations, which have long fueled violence and instability within our borders. In performing this work, the HSTF places special emphasis on investigating and prosecuting those engaged in child trafficking or other crimes involving children. The HSTF further utilizes all available tools to prosecute and remove the most violent criminal aliens from the United States. The Atlanta Wilhelm HSTF comprises agents and officers from ATF, CGIS, DEA, FBI, ICE-HSI, IRS-CI, DOL-OIG, DSS, USMS, USPIS, and USSS, as well as numerous state and local agencies, with the prosecution being led by the U.S. Attorney’s Office for the Northern District of Georgia.

For further information please contact the U.S. Attorney’s Public Affairs Office at USAGAN.PressEmails@usdoj.gov or (404) 581-6185. The Internet address for the U.S. Attorney’s Office for the Northern District of Georgia is http://www.justice.gov/usao-ndga .

